- How would you handle memory pressure or restarts?
- How to test TTL eviction and cross-topic ordering?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of runtime configurability, deduplication logic, and offset management—skills essential for building robust streaming pipelines in production systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Runtime config updates**: Using a dedicated configuration topic to dynamically control sink behavior
- **Per-message deduplication**: Maintaining deduplication state across message processing
- **Toggle behavior**: Safely switching deduplication on/off without losing in-flight messages
- **At-least-once semantics**: Ensuring offsets commit even when deduplication is bypassed
- **Graceful state management**: Handling the transition between dedup-enabled and disabled modes

## Code Example
```javascript
class RuntimeConfig {
  constructor() { this.disableDedup = false; }

  apply(event) {
    if (typeof event.disableDedup === 'boolean') {
      this.disableDedup = event.disableDedup;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions between config updates and incoming messages?
- What happens to deduplication state when the connector restarts?
- How would you implement this using Kafka Streams instead of a sink connector?

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-key stateful streaming logic, rolling window calculations, and safe downstream signaling with offset semantics. It also probes handling of out-of-order arrivals and ensuring at-least-once delivery by committing only after alert emission succeeds.

Key Concepts
- Per-key state management
- Rolling window calculations (60s)
- Threshold-based anomaly detection
- Idempotent downstream writes and offset commits
- Canary test design for spike vs. normal data

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for onMessage
class RuleEngine {
  constructor(windowMs=60000) {
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.state = new Map(); // sensorId -> [{ts,value}, ...], plus sum/count
  }
  onMessage(event){
    const {sensorId, ts, value} = event;
    const bucket = this.state.get(sensorId) || {q:[], sum:0, count:0};
    // prune old
    while (bucket.q.length && bucket.q[0].ts <= ts - this.windowMs) {
      const old = bucket.q.shift(); bucket.sum -= old.value; bucket.count--;
    }
    bucket.q.push({ts,value}); bucket.sum += value; bucket.count++;
    const avg = bucket.count ? bucket.sum / bucket.count : value;
    if (value > 1.2 * avg) { this.emitAlert(sensorId, ts, value, avg); }
    this.state.set(sensorId, bucket);
  }
  rollingAvgFor(sensorId){ const bucket = this.state.get(sensorId); return bucket?.count ? bucket.sum / bucket.count : null; }
  emitAlert(sensorId, ts, value, avg){ // write to threshold_alerts sink
    // sink.write({sensorId, ts, value, avg});
  }
  commitOffsets(){ /* commit after sink write */ }
}
```

